"RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation ofHuman–Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22507v1)\nciteturn41229view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22507v1\",\"lineno\":285}); Total lines: 550\nL248: We first examine how user interaction changes task outcomes across scaffolds, then use matched Claude Code trajectories to connect these changes to collaboration behavior and failure modes.\nL249: \nL250: Figure 4: Pass rate changes by category. Hollow/filled: non-interactive/interactive. Blue/orange: gains/losses ($\\Delta\\mathrm{pp}$, percentage points).\nL251: ### 4.3 Impact of User Interaction on Task Outcomes\nL252: \nL253: Across 11 Claude Code configurations and 101 matched tasks, interactive and autonomous execution achieve comparable mean pass rates (46.8% vs. 47.4%). However, aggregate parity masks substantial bidirectional shifts: 17.9% of runs turn failure into success, while 18.5% turn success into failure. This reordering indicates that user participation does not provide a uniform boost, but depends heavily on how models handle dynamic feedback.\nL254: Scaffold and Task-Domain Sensitivities. The impact of interaction varies significantly across agent harnesses and task domains. Models improve more reliably under Codex (mean gain of 3.4 pp) than under Claude Code or Terminus-2. Furthermore, performance gains concentrate in tasks with local, verifiable requirement additions (e.g., Data Analysis and Software Engineering), where progressive messages supply overlooked boundary conditions.\nL255: Conversely, regressions cluster in tasks requiring strict consistency across existing constraints and incoming requests, where models frequently misinterpret new instructions as global goal replacements rather than local refinements.\nL256: Model Capability Differences in Managing Execution States. Model-level disparities stem fundamentally from how differently models handle dynamic requirements within their execution states. For instance, on streamlit-headless-app-test, despite receiving identical information slices and probes, Kimi K3 improves by 8.9 percentage points by successfully treating late-stage UI and state requirements as persistent constraints over a unified execution path.\nL257: In contrast, HY3 treats these updates as isolated, independent API additions, failing to maintain global state consistency across reruns. This highlights that successful collaboration requires models to reconcile new feedback as continuous constraints on a shared execution state rather than disjointed patches.\nL258: ### 4.4 Multidimensional Collaboration Capabilities in Interactive Tasks\nL259: \nL260: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Collaboration across five dimensions and 22 subdimensions. Colors indicate scenario excellence rates.\nL261: Figure cite81†5 reports scenario excellence rates across five dimensions and 22 subdimensions. To identify the behavior underlying this profile, we examine strict performance on triggered, applicable scenarios. Across all 11 models, the pass rate for updating a plan after an obstacle is 80.2%, compared with 51.7% for recognizing scope expansion. Models respond more reliably to an observed blocker than they recognize that the task goal itself has expanded.\nL262: Models pass 98.8% of scenarios involving successive requirement changes, but only 54.4% for retaining earlier constraints and 29.3% for handling conflicting instructions across turns. They can follow the current request while failing to integrate it with the prior goal state. The main collaboration shortcoming is therefore cross-turn goal maintenance rather than immediate response. Appendix cite47†E.4 provides the scenario-level breakdown.\nL263: ### 4.5 Failure Modes in Task Execution and User Collaboration\nL264: \nL265: Functional or boundary-case errors account for 62.7% of autonomous failures and 53.8% of interactive failures, making them the most common type in both modes (Figure cite82†6 (a)). User participation can change the execution path, but it does not replace verification of boundary conditions, interfaces, and final behavior. The acceptance gap under complete instructions is analyzed in Appendix cite48†E.5 .\nL266: Among successful interactive trajectories, 91.0% still fail at least one applicable collaboration check (Figure cite82†6 (b)). A final artifact can satisfy environment-level verification while requirements for authorization, conflict handling, or risk communication remain unmet. Task completion and collaboration completion therefore require complementary evaluation axes. Appendix cite49†E.6 gives the per-model breakdown.\nL267: Constraint-retention failures further locate this gap between dialogue and delivery. Final artifacts mismatch the stated constraint in 91.3% of these failures. Among artifact mismatches with applicable communication checks, 53.3% pass every communication check. Models can respond appropriately to a constraint in dialogue without realizing it in the final artifact. Appendix cite50†E.7 decomposes these failures.\nL268: Figure 6: Failure patterns. (a) Execution issues among failed runs (categories overlap). (b) Collaboration failure rates by task outcome over triggered, applicable scenarios.\nL269: ## 5 Conclusion\nL270: In this paper, we present RealCLI, a paired benchmark for evaluating task execution and human–agent collaboration of CLI agents on the same realistic tasks. Experiments across 33 configurations show that RealCLI remains challenging for frontier agents, and that strong task outcomes do not imply strong collaboration. Collaboration ability of agents is consistently imbalanced, and models with comparable task success can diverge in how they collaborate.\nL271: These results indicate that outcome-only evaluation conceals systematic collaboration failures, and that advancing CLI agents requires treating collaboration as a first-class, measurable capability.\nL272: ## AI use statement\nL273: \nL274: We use generative AI solely for literature reading and language editing of author-written text. We do not use it for research ideation, experimental design, analysis, generating results or citations, designing surveys or interviews, or creating or modifying figures. We review all AI-assisted content and take full responsibility for the final manuscript.\nL275: ## References\nL276:   * Amershi et al. (2019) S. Amershi, D. S. Weld, M. Vorvoreanu, A. Fourney, B. Nushi, P. Collisson, J. Suh, S. T. Iqbal, P. N. Bennett, K. Inkpen, J. Teevan, R. Kikin-Gil, and E. Horvitz Guidelines for human-ai interaction. In Proceedings of the 2019 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI 2019, Glasgow, Scotland, UK, May 04-09, 2019, S. A. Brewster, G. Fitzpatrick, A. L. Cox, and V. Kostakos (Eds.), pp. 3. External Links: cite83†Link†doi.org , cite84†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite85†§1 .\nL277:   * Bin et al. (2026) Y. Bin, X. Yuan, H. Zeng, W. Ye, W. Shao, Q. Chen, W. Ye, Y. Ding, Z. Wang, P. Zeng, J. Song, and H. T. Shen Terminal agents: A survey of AI agents in command-line environments. CoRR abs/2608.20485. External Links: cite86†Link†doi.org , cite87†Document†dx.doi.org , 2608.20485 Cited by: cite88†§1 , cite89†§1 .\nL278:   * Deng et al. (2025) X. Deng, J. Da, E. Pan, Y. Y. He, C. Ide, K. Garg, N. Lauffer, A. Park, N. Pasari, C. Rane, K. Sampath, M. Krishnan, S. Kundurthy, S. Hendryx, Z. Wang, C. B. C. Zhang, N. Jacobson, B. Liu, and B. Kenstler SWE-bench pro: can AI agents solve long-horizon software engineering tasks?. CoRR abs/2509.16941. External Links: cite90†Link†doi.org , cite91†Document†dx.doi.org , 2509.16941 Cited by: cite92†§A.1 , cite88†§1 .\nL279:   * Dong et al. (2026) G. Dong, X. Song, Y. Hu, J. Jin, C. Zhang, Y. Chen, X. Li, H. Yuan, X. Yang, T. Wen, J. Tan, H. Qian, S. Huang, J. Lu, Z. Li, W. Zhong, Y. Zhu, T. Chua, Z. Dou,